\subsection{Charge a Fused Handler by Guest Instruction}

An execution budget can turn what looks like a private bytecode
boundary into a visible yield point. Take a VM that grants a
worker slices measured in guest operations. A slice of zero returns
control before running the next operation; a return or trap on the
last unit takes precedence over yielding. The dispatcher can still
fuse two pure, nontrapping instructions whose total cost is two.
For this example assume no other inspection points and no branches
into the second operation. If refinement of the schedule permits
arbitrary interruption between host instructions, this fast path
would need a different contract.

\begin{sudocode}[Dispatch under an exact guest budget]
\While{$budget>0$}
  \If{$pc$ starts an eligible pair and $budget\geq 2$}
    \State run pair and advance to next external PC
    \State $budget\gets budget-2$ \Comment{Count guest operations}
  \Else
    \State run exactly one reference operation at $pc$
      \Comment{Bypass fusion for a short slice}
    \State $budget\gets budget-1$
    \If{operation returned or trapped}
      \State \Return its outcome
    \EndIf
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return yielded frame and next external PC
\end{sudocode}

Take \texttt{DUP; ADD; RET} beginning at PC 0, with integer 6 on
the stack and each instruction one byte. A slice of one executes
only \texttt{DUP}, yielding PC 1 and stack $(6,6)$. A second slice
of one yields PC 2 and stack $(12)$. Starting over with a slice
of two permits fusion and must yield the same PC 2 and stack
$(12)$. A slice of three must return 12, not yield a continuation
after the return. Force all four budgets including zero in a
deterministic interpreter test, and track that a paired dispatch
actually ran on the two-unit path.

The invariant at every yield is agreement with an unfused VM
that consumed exactly the granted budget. Fusing reduces
dispatches, not guest charges. If a guest opcode's charge depends
on its operands, a fixed pair cost is insufficient: either compute
the charge without crossing an observation boundary or leave the
pair out of this scheme.

\section{Measuring the whole interpreter}

Compare matched builds with the same guest workload, stack representation, and profiling policy. Record guest operations, host indirect branches and mispredictions where measurable, and instruction-cache pressure. Distinguish predecode time and expansion in bytes from execution gains. A sequence may eliminate millions of dispatches but add enough handler text to slow unrelated hot paths. Use profiles from a training workload to select a bounded set of fusions, then measure on held-out programs; report both bytes of added handler code and improvement in useful work per second.
